# Summary
Introduced. The previous `strip_prefix`-based matching was UTF-8 safe; the commit replaced it with manual `split_at(prefix.len())` prefix extraction without verifying that `prefix.len()` is a character boundary in the attacker-controlled condition key.
The new case-insensitive prefix matching implementation calls `key.split_at(prefix.len())` after checking only the byte length of the policy condition key. Rust string slicing requires the split index to be a valid UTF-8 character boundary. A bucket policy containing a non-ASCII condition key whose bytes cross the prefix-length offset can therefore panic during condition-key lookup. This lookup is reached while validating a PutBucketPolicy request via `validate_evaluable_object_conditions()`, so an authenticated principal with permission to set a bucket policy can make the request handler panic instead of receiving a normal MalformedPolicy error. Under the default Rust unwinding behavior this is likely limited to the connection/task handling the request, but it is still an attacker-triggerable crash path and could become process-wide if built with panic=abort.

# Resolution
Fixed by `1ffb04c7` (`Fix UTF-8 condition key policy validation`). Prefix
condition-key matching now uses UTF-8 boundary-aware `str::get` instead of
slicing with `split_at(prefix.len())`, so policy-controlled non-ASCII keys
whose byte offset crosses a multibyte character simply do not match a prefix
resolver. The duplicate policy-variable key matcher was also removed so policy
variables use the same safe lookup path.

The `PutBucketPolicy` validation path now distinguishes unknown condition keys
from known keys that are unsupported for an action. Unknown keys return the
AWS-pinned `MalformedPolicy` response with message
`Policy has an invalid condition key` and `<Detail>` echoing the offending key.

